Q1. What is Continuous Deployment? Explain how it works.

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Deployment is a DevOps practice in which every successful
build produced by the Continuous Integration (CI) pipeline is
automatically deployed to the production environment without any manual
approval. Every code change that passes all automated tests is released
to end users immediately.

Definition

Continuous Deployment is the practice of automatically deploying every
successful build to the production environment without manual
intervention.

Purpose

• Eliminate manual deployment.
• Release software immediately.
• Deliver bug fixes and features faster.
• Reduce release time.
• Achieve a fully automated software delivery pipeline.

======================================================================

Q2. Explain the workflow of Continuous Deployment.

Interview Ready Answer

After developers commit code, the CI pipeline automatically builds and
tests the application. If all tests pass, the deployment pipeline
immediately deploys the application to the production environment
without requiring manual approval.

Workflow

Developer Writes Code
        │
        ▼
Commit & Push
        │
        ▼
Continuous Integration
(Build + Test)
        │
        ▼
All Tests Pass
        │
        ▼
Automatic Deployment
        │
        ▼
Production

Important Point

There is NO manual approval before production deployment.

======================================================================

Q3. Why is Continuous Deployment considered risky?

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Deployment is considered risky because every successful build
is automatically deployed to production without manual approval. If
automated tests fail to detect a defect, faulty code may reach end users
immediately. Therefore, Continuous Deployment requires highly reliable
automated testing.

Reasons

• No manual approval.
• Bugs can directly reach production.
• Depends completely on automated testing.
• Incorrect tests may allow faulty software to be released.
• Immediate impact on end users.

======================================================================

Q4. In which applications should Continuous Deployment be avoided?

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Deployment should generally be avoided in safety-critical and
highly regulated applications where manual verification, compliance
checks, or business approval are mandatory before production release.

Examples

• Banking systems.
• Healthcare applications.
• Aviation software.
• Government systems.
• Payment gateways.
• Financial trading platforms.

Reason

These applications require strict validation because deployment failures
can lead to financial loss, legal issues, or safety risks.

